---@class aws.transcribe.UpdateMedicalVocabularyRequest
---@field VocabularyName aws.transcribe.VocabularyName The name of the custom medical vocabulary you want to update. Custom medical vocabulary names are case sensitive.
---@field LanguageCode aws.transcribe.LanguageCode|string The language code that represents the language of the entries in the custom vocabulary you want to update. US English (en-US) is the only language supported with Amazon Transcribe Medical.
---@field VocabularyFileUri aws.transcribe.Uri The Amazon S3 location of the text file that contains your custom medical vocabulary. The URI must be located in the same Amazon Web Services Region as the resource you're calling.

---@class aws.transcribe.UpdateMedicalVocabularyResponse
---@field VocabularyName? aws.transcribe.VocabularyName The name of the updated custom medical vocabulary.
---@field LanguageCode? aws.transcribe.LanguageCode The language code you selected for your custom medical vocabulary. US English (en-US) is the only language supported with Amazon Transcribe Medical.
---@field LastModifiedTime? aws.transcribe.DateTime The date and time the specified custom medical vocabulary was last updated.
---@field VocabularyState? aws.transcribe.VocabularyState The processing state of your custom medical vocabulary. If the state is READY, you can use the custom vocabulary in a StartMedicalTranscriptionJob request.

---@class aws.transcribe.UpdateVocabularyFilterRequest
---@field VocabularyFilterName aws.transcribe.VocabularyFilterName The name of the custom vocabulary filter you want to update. Custom vocabulary filter names are case sensitive.
---@field Words? aws.transcribe.Words Use this parameter if you want to update your custom vocabulary filter by including all desired terms, as comma-separated values, within your request. The other option for updating your vocabulary filter is to save your entries in a text file and upload them to an Amazon S3 bucket, then specify the location of your file using the VocabularyFilterFileUri parameter.
---@field VocabularyFilterFileUri? aws.transcribe.Uri The Amazon S3 location of the text file that contains your custom vocabulary filter terms. The URI must be located in the same Amazon Web Services Region as the resource you're calling.
---@field DataAccessRoleArn? aws.transcribe.DataAccessRoleArn The Amazon Resource Name (ARN) of an IAM role that has permissions to access the Amazon S3 bucket that contains your input files (in this case, your custom vocabulary filter). If you include EncryptionConfiguration in your request, this role must also have permissions to access the specified KMS key. If the role that you specify doesn’t have the appropriate permissions, your request fails.
---@field EncryptionConfiguration? aws.transcribe.EncryptionConfiguration Specifies the new encryption configuration for your custom vocabulary filter. The vocabulary filter artifacts are re-encrypted in place using the specified KMS key or with an AWS-owned key if a key is not supplied.

---@class aws.transcribe.UpdateVocabularyFilterResponse
---@field VocabularyFilterName? aws.transcribe.VocabularyFilterName The name of the updated custom vocabulary filter.
---@field LanguageCode? aws.transcribe.LanguageCode The language code you selected for your custom vocabulary filter.
---@field LastModifiedTime? aws.transcribe.DateTime The date and time the specified custom vocabulary filter was last updated.

---@class aws.transcribe.UpdateVocabularyRequest
---@field VocabularyName aws.transcribe.VocabularyName The name of the custom vocabulary you want to update. Custom vocabulary names are case sensitive.
---@field LanguageCode aws.transcribe.LanguageCode|string The language code that represents the language of the entries in the custom vocabulary you want to update. Each custom vocabulary must contain terms in only one language.
---@field Phrases? aws.transcribe.Phrases Use this parameter if you want to update your custom vocabulary by including all desired terms, as comma-separated values, within your request. The other option for updating your custom vocabulary is to save your entries in a text file and upload them to an Amazon S3 bucket, then specify the location of your file using the VocabularyFileUri parameter.
---@field VocabularyFileUri? aws.transcribe.Uri The Amazon S3 location of the text file that contains your custom vocabulary. The URI must be located in the same Amazon Web Services Region as the resource you're calling.
---@field DataAccessRoleArn? aws.transcribe.DataAccessRoleArn The Amazon Resource Name (ARN) of an IAM role that has permissions to access the Amazon S3 bucket that contains your input files (in this case, your custom vocabulary). If you include EncryptionConfiguration in your request, this role must also have permissions to access the specified KMS key. If the role that you specify doesn’t have the appropriate permissions, your request fails.
---@field EncryptionConfiguration? aws.transcribe.EncryptionConfiguration Specifies the new encryption configuration for your custom vocabulary. The vocabulary artifacts are re-encrypted in place using the specified KMS key or with an AWS-owned key if a key is not supplied.

---@class aws.transcribe.UpdateVocabularyResponse
